# lxgr-dump

Standalone physical-address memory dumper for ARM64 Android (root). Dumps
mapped regions of a running process, resolves pointer chains to absolute
virtual addresses, and maps every dumped page to its **physical address** via
`/proc/<pid>/pagemap` — a PA dump that standard tools (Il2CppDumper,
GameGuardian, /proc/pid/mem dumpers) do not produce.

Works standalone as a CLI, or as a tiny library linked into any other project
(the LXGR overlay compiles `lxgr_dump.c` in as an extension).

## Build

Requires the Android NDK. From this directory:

```sh
ndk-build NDK_PROJECT_PATH=. APP_BUILD_SCRIPT=Android.mk NDK_APPLICATION_MK=Application.mk
# -> libs/arm64-v8a/lxgr_dump
```

## Usage

All reads require root (`su`). Dumps are written as a raw binary plus a
`<out>.pamap` physical map (`# VA PA` per page, `-` for non-present).

```sh
# Dump every readable segment of libil2cpp.so, reconstructed at file offsets:
lxgr_dump --pid <pid> --lib libil2cpp.so --out /data/local/tmp/libil2cpp.so --pamap

# Raw virtual range:
lxgr_dump --pid <pid> --base 0x4102d0 --size 0x1000 --out /data/local/tmp/range.bin --pamap

# Resolve a pointer chain to an absolute VA (+ print its PA):
#   B = libil2cpp base, auto-discovered.  base+0x10 -> *(x) -> *(x+0x8) -> +0x20
lxgr_dump --pid <pid> --chain "B+0x10>0x8>0x20"

# Dump starting at a resolved chain target:
lxgr_dump --pid <pid> --chain "B+0x10>0x8" --size 0x1000 --out /data/local/tmp/target.bin

# Address conversion only:
lxgr_dump --pid <pid> --v2p 0x4102d0
lxgr_dump --pid <pid> --p2v 0x57fd012d0
```

## Library API

`lxgr_dump.c` + `lxgr_dump.h` — no dependencies beyond libc. Link it into any
codebase to get:

```c
uint64_t lxgr_va_to_pa(int pid, uint64_t va);            /* VA -> PA (pagemap) */
uint64_t lxgr_pa_to_va(int pid, uint64_t pa);            /* PA -> VA (scan)     */
uint64_t lxgr_resolve_chain(int pid, uint64_t base, const char *spec);
long     lxgr_dump_range(int pid, uint64_t va, uint64_t size, int fd, uint64_t off);
int      lxgr_pamap_range(int pid, uint64_t va, uint64_t size, FILE *pf);
```

`lxgr_read()` prefers `process_vm_readv` and falls back to `/proc/<pid>/mem`.

## Notes

- `PA` here is the physical page containing the data. `/dev/mem` is blocked on
  most kernels (STRICT_DEVMEM), so true byte-for-byte physical reads require a
  kernel-side primitive; the overlay's rwbridge module (independent) is the
  optional backend for that.
- Requires root: pagemap needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN; cross-process reads need
  CAP_SYS_PTRACE.
